1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,680
DGP is supposed to be stateless.

2
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:13,480
So each request that I send should have no impact on the next request.

3
00:00:13,580 --> 00:00:15,980
And obviously request smuggling violates that.

4
00:00:15,980 --> 00:00:18,800
But even if you ignore request smuggling,

5
00:00:18,920 --> 00:00:22,520
there are some other cases when HP can become stateful.

6
00:00:23,180 --> 00:00:25,960
And if you don't realize this and test for it,

7
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:28,320
you can miss some quite interesting vulnerabilities.

8
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:35,320
For example, some servers do more validation on the first request

9
00:00:35,980 --> 00:00:38,260
sent over any given connection.

10
00:00:38,260 --> 00:00:44,940
So on this target, there's an SSRF vulnerability.

11
00:00:45,660 --> 00:00:49,840
And now if we try to hit this internal host directly,

12
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:53,540
well, they detect that the host header is wrong

13
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:56,520
and they just issue us with a redirect.

14
00:00:56,520 --> 00:01:01,420
And you can send that over and over and you'll get the same thing every time.

15
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:07,340
But the thing is, this server is only doing the validation of the host header

16
00:01:07,340 --> 00:01:10,420
on the first request on each connection.

17
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:16,020
So I've just made a custom action called connection state attack,

18
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:19,880
which basically takes this and it smuggles it behind a valid,

19
00:01:19,880 --> 00:01:22,040
genuine, normal looking request.

20
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:24,700
And so all you've got to do is first send this,

21
00:01:24,740 --> 00:01:27,180
see what the normal response is and then hit this button.

22
00:01:27,180 --> 00:01:29,260
And now it will smuggle the request.

23
00:01:30,420 --> 00:01:36,460
And behold, we've just gained access to the admin panel via the SSRF.

24
00:01:36,680 --> 00:01:40,820
And once again, the only way to access this is by smuggling this request.

25
00:01:40,820 --> 00:01:43,240
So if you do it directly, you don't see anything.

26
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:47,920
We can use the logger to see what's actually happened behind the scene.

27
00:01:48,040 --> 00:01:51,880
So when I'm sending these requests with the repeater,

28
00:01:52,120 --> 00:01:55,480
every single one is sent on a different connection ID,

29
00:01:55,600 --> 00:01:57,840
as you can see from this column that I've switched on here.

30
00:01:58,280 --> 00:02:00,780
But when I hit that magic button with the custom action,

31
00:02:01,180 --> 00:02:05,300
then it sends this innocent precursor request with a valid host header.

32
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:10,400
And then it sends the malformed one that I had in the repeater.

33
00:02:11,220 --> 00:02:17,800
And the power of this is this is not just limited to host header attacks.

34
00:02:17,980 --> 00:02:27,180
In principle, any kind of server level validation could be bypassed using this technique.

35
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:31,320
And now thanks to this custom action, you can test for it simply by pressing a button.

36
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:36,060
If we just have a look at the code underneath,

37
00:02:36,660 --> 00:02:40,020
all it's doing is it's taking the input request

38
00:02:40,020 --> 00:02:46,360
and it's sending a simplified version to the target URL with a legitimate host header

39
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:50,140
and without any malformed headers or anything like that.

40
00:02:50,280 --> 00:02:55,820
And then it's sending the actual attack immediately after it and setting the response over there.

41
00:02:56,600 --> 00:02:59,020
So, yeah, hope you find it useful.

42
00:02:59,020 --> 00:03:04,880
Please note you will need to be using the latest version of Burp Suite to perform this attack

43
00:03:04,880 --> 00:03:13,120
as it uses a new feature that was just added to around making it easier to reuse connections like this.

44
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:15,840
And that's pretty much it.

45
00:03:16,340 --> 00:03:21,140
Only other thing is this is my first time making this kind of demo video with audio narration.

46
00:03:21,360 --> 00:03:26,060
So let me know what you think if you like it or if you think I should just stick to writing blog posts.

47
00:03:26,760 --> 00:03:27,580
Thanks for listening.

48
00:03:29,020 --> 00:03:31,020
Thank you.

